# Local critical reduction for a nonbinary symplectic MS-pair

The whole moving fixed kernels at both critical endpoints now lie in the
full critical commutator. Its exact order identifies the reciprocal
critical indices. On the actual large-mover terminal paths, the retained
central intersections are these entire kernels, repeating after two
backward shifts. At distance six both alternating kernels agree. The
remaining unequal-contained selectors, normalizer comparison, terminal
exclusions and actual distance-two replacement witness remain open.

The strict moving-index theorem (5.9) now gives nonnormality at the literal
first two-step neighbor. Its actual residual-orbit shifts preserve every
path overlap and every equal/unequal-mover comparison. In the full
point-mover branch, the same selected individual movers generate the
vertices and retain the original whole-edge fixed subgroups, placing the
complete R₄ edge intersection in three full vertex centers. The unequal
contained selector clauses, subsequent terminal order comparisons and
exclusions, and actual minimum-replacement distance-two witness remain.

The contained large-endpoint branch now has paired generation in its
central-intersection case. The actual marked T₀ and endpoint generate the
rank-one image. One shear in the original plane radical makes T₀, its
conjugate and the endpoint generate the whole point local; the unequal
mover then gives the full symplectic group. The same residual lift supplies
both identities through the entire Q-kernel, retaining the original and
transported plane markings, neighbor, root pair, intersection bounds,
whole-X nonsplitting and whole-edge fixed-subgroup comparison. The complete
individual/paired selector, compatible paths, terminal exclusions and
minimum-characteristic distance-two witness remain to be established.

The critical-root case now constructs a genuine moving neighbor and its
complete original source selector, without a strict B-relative index premise.
Vertex and edge transitivity supply an index at least q. If any neighbor has
index greater than q, the established (5.7) selector applies. Otherwise the
uniform bound lets the terminal twist exclude trapping of the very seed chosen
by the equal-mover, unequal-mover or full-point selector. Individual residual
generation, both paired generation identities, whole-Z fixed subgroups, the
full quotient marking and every backward-path overlap are retained.

The local twist argument only requires the selected seed to be trapped. The
literal terminal intersection and the same residual twist preserve its order-q
source commutator. Full-cover hyperplane supplementation identifies it with the
whole-U commutator of the transformed seed. Containment in the original critical
root and symplectic fixed-kernel duality force it to vanish. The full-radius
nontrapping result and its public interfaces remain available. Larger critical
images still require their complete source-selector compatibility; terminal
exclusions and the actual minimum-replacement distance-two witness remain open.

The minimum characteristic replacement has a positive even critical distance,
its vertex centralizer is exactly the neighborhood kernel, and its natural
cover carries the full faithful semilinear vertex action at the original field
degree. Its intrinsic commutator core is the original component core. The
original odd-index witness supplies a full component Sylow normalized by the
replacement radical; its literal fixed seed has the required size bound and
centralizer containment. Both critical indices are at least the field size,
and each critical image is inner. The full fixed-Sylow seed lies in the source
critical radius; its star is abelian and acts quadratically when the distance
is at least four. In this branch the full holomorph star lies in the join of
the moving layer and fixed core, so the characteristic cut removes outer action.

The reciprocal critical comparison now works at every nonbinary degree:
an index below q² forces both indices to equal q. An escaping seed excludes
the large-image branch by the orthogonal bound 2q < q², and its actual root
hyperplane gives fixed index at least q on the full endpoint intersection.
The application to a full-Sylow seed in the source star discharges its
start-core containment using the radius bound.

The escaping seed is now selected at arbitrary degree: a primitive prime of
binary order 4n excludes nontrivial component actions on its q² section.
The actual bad star yields both field-sized critical indices and movement of
the full endpoint intersection. An elementary actor normalizing a full Sylow
seed acts on that entire seed through field automorphisms, so its centralizer
index is at most n. Since n < q, this excludes an escaping star seed in both
penultimate branches and proves [Z-star-alpha,L-alpha] <= U-alpha. Transport
of the seeds also puts the complete holomorph star in the critical endpoint's
core on a path through the marked edge. An actual constructor supplies the
geometry, seed and path together with these comparisons.

The source star has core displacement in the full residual-fixed core. For
distance at least four it is elementary, with moved subgroup exactly U.
Its actual quotient by the fixed core is a dual cover of the original natural
module. Critical actors exclude invariant quadratic refinements in both the
root and large-image cases, so endpoint centrality proves the second star
bound Z-star-beta <= U-alpha D-alpha at every nonbinary degree.

These results supply outer removal on the replacement used in Section 7:
its distance is two, or is at least four and its ambient quotient is inner.
The whole stabilizer consequently has an actual inner symplectic quotient
and compatible natural cover at every original-group vertex. Every normalized
radius section of order below q⁴ is fixed by the residual; MS3 therefore forces
the radius quotient to have order at least q⁴. At every distance at least four,
including four itself, its image within critical range has order at most q³ and its full kernel quotient has
order at least q. A large critical image gives fixed index at least q² on
each noncentral chief factor, so the literal radius quotient has exactly
one such factor. The residual alternative and abelian edge generation then
prove radius centrality, the derived-radius bound and the critical triple
commutator of MS93 (5.2). Every quotient retains its full central seed.

At a critical image of order at least q², an actual escaping neighboring
conjugate now constructs both backward and forward critical shifts, retaining
all overlap vertices. The general choice yields either a backward shift or
a field-sized root case with quadratic action and fixed index at least q on
the full endpoint intersection. The radius modulo the entire fixed preimage
B has order at least q⁴, and every nearby kernel has index at least q modulo B.
If the radius is trapped in the penultimate kernel, its endpoint image has
order strictly greater than q; the same radius and full intersection supply
the quadratic root obstruction.

The special Thompson/parabolic choices in (5.5)--(5.6), the remaining root
selection and strict-index argument, and the terminal exclusions remain unproved.
The small-mover family used by these choices is now classified at every
original field degree, using its exact action on the full U-layer. Either
there is a unique maximum mover, or there are q+1, their join is the whole
replacement edge group, and its quotient by the vertex kernel has order q³.
In the latter case the same surjective vertex marking identifies the edge
image with the literal symplectic point radical. A containing Sylow is
constructed without assuming the replacement edge is itself Sylow. The
root-product exclusion (5.1) now holds in the multiple-maximum branch.
A hypothetical failed exclusion forces a unique maximum of relative order
at most q² and normalization of both full root-fixed seeds. The ordinary
all-abelian Thompson subgroup of the replacement escapes Q; Thompson
replacement selects a maximum abelian actor outside Q which is quadratic
on the full U-layer. The exact cover indices make it a genuine small mover,
so the actual small-mover join also escapes Q. At a field-sized critical
pair the final-edge small-mover join normalizes the full endpoint
intersection Z_end intersect Q_start. These are literal source inputs,
including all fixed central attachments. The Niles pushing-up step in the
unique-maximum branch of (5.1), the maximizing parabolic choice and the
terminal exclusions remain unproved.
No upper distance bound or replacement odd-index radical witness is asserted here.
Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher (1993), pp.868--869, 877--878, 912--926,
932--933; Aschbacher--Smith C.1.31--32, pp.135--136.
The root-product order bound also controls every maximum abelian subgroup,
so the ordinary Thompson and Baumann subgroups of the actual edge lie in
its unique maximum small mover. MS3 makes that Baumann subgroup escape Q;
it normalizes both full root-fixed seeds. The product premise now identifies
the actual small-mover join with the join of the two source roots. Explicit
inner conjugation and a matrix rank-one overgroup construct F inside the
original stabilizer, with that full join as a Sylow subgroup and with quotient
SL₂ at the original field degree. The entire Q-kernel is retained. The
two-residual of F now moves the original Thompson central omega, proving
clause (iii). Its Sylow has exactly the original Baumann subgroup and
Thompson central omega, and the same full-kernel marking places F in a
concrete point parabolic. Alignment with the selected path, parabolic
generation, the Baumann-Sylow construction F₀ and Niles pushing-up remain open.
The active root can now be selected using the original Thompson subgroup,
so both it and the Baumann subgroup escape O₂(F). The literal Baumann
normal closure C supplements O₂(F), has the same original-degree SL₂
quotient, and satisfies F*(C)=O₂(C). Its actual Sylow is the intersection
with the original small-mover join and has a unique maximal overgroup.
Identifying that intersection with the original Baumann subgroup remains
the missing Sylow clause in the F₀ construction.

The q² bound now makes every maximum abelian offender sharp on the full
natural layer. Consequently each (A intersect Q-alpha) U-alpha is a maximum
abelian replacement, and the full maximum family of Q-alpha remains in
the original edge's family. The original Thompson central omega joined
with U-alpha is elementary, normal in the actual vertex stabilizer, and
contained in Q-alpha. Its residual displacement lies in U-alpha; more
precisely, the moved subgroup of Ω₁(Z(J(Q-alpha))) is exactly U-alpha.
The literal root-product premise supplies these results without additional
module assumptions. The enlarged layer is now contained in the original
Baumann subgroup and hence in its normal closure N. The actual Sylow of N
retains the original all-abelian central omega and Baumann subgroup, and
N has the same whole two-residual as the envelope. Its canonical ordinary
Sylow-center closure is elementary, lies in Ω₁Z(O₂(N)), splits into its
moved and fixed parts, and is still moved by the residual. Both this module
and the whole core omega have centralizer exactly O₂(N), giving faithful
SL₂ action at the original field degree. The Baumann-Sylow equality,
selected-path generation, Niles conclusion and terminal exclusions remain open.
The native Baumann subgroup now has the full Sylow image at the original
field degree: P = B O₂(N). It supplements the whole two-residual, and an
actual residual conjugator gives two Baumann conjugates generating modulo
O₂(N). The two Thompson central omega conjugates intersect exactly in their
full central part. These generator identities do not identify P with B;
centralization of the original Thompson omega by O₂(N) is still required.

The nonzero residual layer D = [U-alpha,O²(N)] now equals the residual
commutator of the enlarged Thompson omega layer. The smaller Ω₁Z(J(P))D
is elementary and normal in the actual Baumann closure and contains its
entire Thompson omega normal closure. Core centralization of D and fixed
index bounds at most q for Baumann and at least q² for the whole group
would identify P with the original Baumann subgroup. The checked implication
uses the actual residual opposite and literal subgroup inclusions; these
three remaining local premises are not asserted.

The Baumann-Sylow construction is now complete on the same closure. The
root-product join acts quadratically on the entire original U-layer.
Quadratic normal generation centralizes D by the whole two-core, and the
opposite commutators span D inside the corresponding Thompson omegas.
This proves Baumann equals the constructed Sylow without the numerical
fixed-index premises. The resulting subgroup F₀ lies in the original
stabilizer, supplements O₂(F), retains the whole residual and has quotient
SL₂ at the original field degree. Selected-path generation and the absence
of characteristic normal subgroups, arbitrary-degree Niles, terminal
exclusions and the distance-two witness remain unproved.

The characteristic-normal exclusion now holds for this same F₀. The original
odd-index witness supplies a full Sylow normalizing every replacement edge.
In its quotient coordinates a normal pair of full transvection roots must
be the central long root. Since the F₀ Sylow is nonnormal, F₀ together with
the full Sylow preimage generates the vertex stabilizer. Every characteristic
subgroup of the original Baumann Sylow normal in F₀ is consequently normal
at both edge vertices and is trivial by MS3. The envelope, supplement and
whole-residual identities are retained in this single witness. The Niles
conclusion at the original degree, subsequent selected paths and terminal
exclusions, and the distance-two witness still remain to be established.

The same F₀ now has its literal two-core identified as F₀ intersect Q-alpha.
Its full original U-alpha lies in the central omega of that core. The
original Baumann Sylow has additive-field quotient of order q; two of its
conjugates, selected inside O²(F₀), generate F₀ and meet exactly in O₂(F₀).
Every actor outside this core has fixed index at least q on its full
central omega. Maximum abelian order makes the bound sharp and supplies
the replacements of Niles 7.4, an actual quadratic offender, and the
invariant-kernel intersection selection of 7.5 at every original degree.
The larger invariant-kernel omega now has Sylow centralizer exactly the
invariant kernel. Niles 7.5's actual maximum abelian subgroup has fixed
index q on this layer, and its residual conjugate gives a supplement with
fixed index at most q squared. The original-degree SL2 representation
bound supplies the matching lower bound on the core omega. The literal
cyclic-orbit formula of Niles 7.6 follows. The same F0 consequently has
a central-chief quotient satisfying all Main Hypothesis inputs, with
its original residual centralizing the removed kernel and its new
core quotient explicitly SL2 at the unchanged field degree.
The original-degree quotient now has its actual residual Sylow pair and
fixed-normal-free intersection diagram. Finite descent constructs an
elementary maximum abelian row, proves involution generation and elementary
centers, and reconstructs the exact center cells through depth 2v. The
abelian-core branch has Sylow class at most two, and this bound lifts to
the original Baumann Sylow through the residual-centralized kernel.
Full center covering now holds at the original degree: the core-center
commutator with the Sylow, joined with the ambient center, is the entire
Sylow center. The two full center indices are q and q². The same quotient
has a residual pair with reconstruction through depth 3v, elementary
short intervals, exact center markings, endpoint-deletion normality and
commutator bounds, opposite-point kernels and right-interval centralizers.
The nonabelian structure argument, elementwise covering, source commutator
conclusion, root-product exclusion, selected paths, terminal exclusions
and distance-two witness assembly remain with this reduction.

The same F₀ central-chief quotient now has Niles's two ambient-normal gap
layers. Its three factors are elementary, and the middle factor has order
q² with action kernel exactly O₂ at the original field degree. In the
class-two branch the core modulo its full center is ambient central.
The noncentral gap now satisfies 5v = 2k and v is even. The same original
quotient has natural coordinates on its full core center modulo all global
fixed vectors, intertwining the original SL₂ marking at the unchanged field
degree. The upper noncentral factor now has literal M/N coordinates for the
same original marking, completing both natural factors of Niles 5.5 and
retaining all ambient central factors. The remaining nonabelian structure,
source commutator conclusion, root-product exclusion, selected paths,
terminal exclusions and distance-two witness remain to be established.


The same original quotient now has the Niles half-period reduction.
Either its Sylow has class at most two, or the actual power automorphism
produces a central-chief quotient retaining class three and the original
SL₂ marking. A literal terminal triple commutator survives, including its
two-step escape under the induced twist. This forces the new diagram
parameter to be exactly two while leaving the original field degree
unchanged. The characteristic-two class-three branch is now excluded:
the literal core modulo the whole ambient center has two marked natural
factors and a fixed section of order q². The binary extension argument makes
the Sylow quadratic on that quotient, and characteristic normal exclusion
forces class at most two. This bound lifts through the residual-centralized
kernel to the original Baumann subgroup. Its full center is elementary,
and the class-two Thompson enlargement gives the original core commutator
conclusion. The same rank-one residual moves U-alpha. Simplicity of the
whole vertex quotient extends its centralization of Q-alpha/U-alpha to the
vertex residual, contradicting radius movement and MS3. Thus the root-product
exclusion (5.1) holds at every original nonbinary degree. It excludes the
single-root maximum in (5.4), so the unique maximum has relative order at
least q squared and retains its precise wing or finite-plane coordinates.
The entire maximum-small-mover join has relative order at least q squared
in both branches. The full X-alpha fixed quotient now has a literal natural dual cover,
with exact indices for its own vertex marking. The large small-mover join
contains a quadratic maximum of order at least q squared, so it preserves
no quadratic refinement. Its full centralizer in X-alpha therefore lies
in U-alpha D-alpha, proving (5.3) without removing any central attachment.
The maximizing parabolic is now constructed in one whole-vertex marking.
Its full preimage contains the replacement edge, its actual two-core acts
quadratically on the entire U-layer, and its intersection with the full
small-mover join is maximal among all conjugate plane-radical preimages.
A small-index overgroup in the same edge equals that join. The generation
and fixed-layer clauses of (5.5)--(5.6), selected paths, terminal exclusions
and the distance-two witness remain open.

The full-plane, field-sized-critical-image branch now has the simultaneous
source selector at every original degree. The literal small-mover join of
relative order q cubed equals the chosen parabolic radical. The same two
original residual elements give source generation, exact common fixed
subgroup on the full Z-layer, and membership of the selector in the chosen
parabolic. The proof retains the entire Q-kernel and every central factor.
Smaller movers, larger critical images, the remaining source choices and
terminal exclusions, and the distance-two witness are still unproved.

The opening separation in (5.5) now holds for both source alternatives:
unequal neighboring small-mover joins, or a join outside the chosen plane
radical, have combined fixed index greater than q² on the full U-layer.
The root-branch fixed-layer and parabolic-positioning clauses of (5.6)
now use the same actual Q₁ without a cubic-mover assumption. In the chosen
parabolic branch the smaller join is exactly the edge intersection with Q₁,
and contains the field-sized endpoint with a strictly larger quotient image.
It has the same fixed subgroup as Q₁ on the full U- and Z-layers. The same
selector consequently gives the full Z fixed-layer clause for the two smaller
join conjugates, with all central attachments retained. In the field-sized
critical-image branch, generation by the smaller join is now proved for the
same pair giving whole-Z fixed-layer equality and original parabolic placement.
The proof keeps the literal J ≤ Q₁ branch and the original maximizing marking;
a plane-preserving shear and rank-one torus supply generation at every
nonbinary degree. For a join of relative order q cubed, the larger-image selector now gives
one-conjugate generation and whole-plane-pair generation through the same
full kernel. Together with the root selector this covers every image size
in the cubic branch, including the full Z fixed-subgroup equality and the
source parabolic positioning when required. In the cubic-mover large-image
branch, the same residual selector now constructs an actual backward critical
path with the exact common edge and every overlap vertex. MS3 discharges the
neighbor nonnormality, and no equality of neighboring movers is required.
The proper-join large-image selector, the remaining unequal-mover selectors,
root-image selected paths and terminal exclusions remain required for the
distance-two witness.

The unequal-neighbor root branch now has generation by the literal join
of both movers. Full-Sylow normalization of the original mover puts its
small image in the standard plane; an arbitrary-degree long-root and torus
calculation makes the cross-join generate with the critical endpoint.
The same residual selector retains the actual two movers at the new edge,
the exact U-index modulo B and fixed equality on the full original edge Z.
It gives an overlapping backward critical path or the full trapped-root
alternative. Generation by either individual mover, proper-J large-image
selection, trapped-root exclusion and terminal distance exclusions remain.

In the full point-mover branch, a large quadratic critical image now gives
one-conjugate generation at every original field degree. Together with the
root selector this covers every critical-image size, using the same residual
element for all neighboring movers. The selected neighbor retains its full
edge-Z fixed subgroup and exact B-relative U-index. At an image larger than
the field, trapping would force field-sized reciprocal images, so this same
neighbor supplies the actual backward critical path with every overlap.
The path still has the original distance; no distance-two witness is asserted.

For a smaller join inside the chosen plane core, a critical image outside
that plane is now identified with a full point-plane subgroup of order q
squared. Its full long root also lies in J. The smaller-root calculation
therefore supplies generation by J itself, whole-Z fixed equality and
parabolic position for the same residual pair. In the equal-neighbor branch,
this pair gives an actual backward critical path with the exact B-relative
U-index and all overlaps. The large endpoint contained in the chosen plane,
individual unequal-mover selectors, trapped-root and terminal exclusions,
and the minimum-replacement distance-two witness remain unproved.

Each individual unequal neighboring mover now has at least q squared
images and fixed index exactly q cubed on the original U-layer. One
full-kernel vertex marking identifies its fixed space with the standard
point and proves it nonquadratic, with normalization by the actual edge
image. It fixes exactly the same subgroup of the whole U-layer as that
edge. This strengthens the cross-join separation without assuming original
kernel containment in the individual mover. Unequal movers are each the unique
maximum on their own whole U-layer, with exact fixed index q squared and
quadratic action there. An individual image of order at most q squared now
forces the entire original Q-kernel into that mover and gives both exact
square image orders and the intersection bound. These conclusions persist
at the same actual root-selected neighbor, with its original B-relative
index, whole edge-Z fixed subgroup for the join, and path-or-trapped
alternative. Individual generation, its exceptional nonsplitting clause,
contained large-image smaller-J selection, and terminal exclusions remain
required.


The unequal-neighbor root selector now also has the source fixed-layer
clause for the original individual mover at the selected edge. The same
conjugator generates with the join of both movers and makes the two full
plane cores generate. Whole-Z fixed equality lifts this to the entire
original edge Z, without discarding any central attachment. The actual
selected neighbor retains the quadratic own-layer actions, cubic movement
on the other layer, full-kernel small-image alternative, B-relative index,
and backward-path or trapped-root data. The small-image alternative now
also forces the genuine source nonsplitting X != U C_X(G_alpha). Conjugate
movers have equal central omega orders, which conflict with their square
and cubic fixed indices on U if this decomposition exists. The same selected
neighbor retains this conclusion. Individual generation, contained large-image
smaller-J selection, trapped-root and terminal exclusions, and the distance-two
witness remain unproved.

In the field-sized critical-root case, the full-X movement bound and four
root conjugates now give X = U C_X(G_alpha) at every original nonbinary
degree. This excludes the saturated unequal-mover exception in that case:
the neighboring mover misses the original Q-kernel and has image strictly
larger than q squared. Both conclusions hold at the same selected neighbor,
with its full edge-Z fixed equalities and all path data. Individual residual
generation, the contained large-image smaller-J selector, trapped-root and
terminal exclusions, and the actual distance-two witness remain required.

The root-case individual residual-generation clause now holds at the same
actual selected neighbor. Its image is strictly larger than the original
plane intersection. Full-Sylow normalization either makes it the entire
edge image or forces more than q central elements into it, giving individual
root generation at every original degree. The missing Q-kernel is handled
by its normalization of this mover: the normal closure lies in the generated
subgroup and has two-group quotient, so the whole vertex residual lies
there. The same selector retains both whole-edge-Z fixed equalities, the
B-relative U-index, quadratic own-layer actions, strict image growth and
all backward-path overlaps or the trapped-root alternative. Larger critical
images with their genuine nonsplitting exception, contained large-image
smaller-J selection, trapped-root and terminal exclusions, and the minimum
characteristic distance-two witness remain open.


For unequal movers with a nonplane critical image, the same actual selected
neighbor has individual residual generation, joined generation, both whole-edge-Z
fixed equalities and a backward critical path with every overlap. The full
nonplane endpoint puts the Sylow center in the normalized edge image. The
individual mover meets that center in at least q elements, which now gives
generation even when it contains Q. This retains the genuine whole-X
nonsplitting conclusion in that branch. The endpoint has order q squared,
so the trapped-root alternative is excluded. Contained large-image smaller-J
selection, the remaining contained-image individual generation, root trapping,
terminal exclusions and the minimum replacement distance-two witness remain
open. Original-radical characteristic control is still a separate obligation.

For the contained endpoint and its actual unequal neighboring mover, the
central-intersection branch now forces the full original Q into the mover.
Source (5.1) excludes the missing-kernel case through the marked index-two
root pair. The same residual selector retains the original and transported
intersection bounds, whole-X nonsplitting, every marked Sylow/root pair,
and whole-edge-Z fixed-subgroup comparison. The local T₀-conjugate
generation, complete individual/paired selector, compatible paths,
terminal exclusions and minimum-replacement witness remain unproved.


The contained unequal-mover selector now also has generation by each actual
selected edge and the whole endpoint, on the same u,v,x and both original
quotient markings. A proper point-local supplement would contain the actual
smaller J in a square-order root pair, excluded by (5.1). Quotient transport
and the full Q-kernel give the second edge identity without assuming that
the shear centralizes the whole endpoint. Both selected neighboring seeds
then escape the penultimate kernel and supply backward critical paths,
retaining every overlap, both B-relative indices, both whole-Z fixed clauses,
and the individual/paired generation and nonsplitting alternatives.
The original J <= Q1 and neighbor-nonnormality premises are retained. Full
Lambda/strict-index assembly, terminal exclusions and the minimum-replacement
distance-two witness remain to be proved.

The strict-index centralizer T₀ = C_Qa(U_c intersect B_a) now retains its
literal definition at both selected vertices on the same v and x. A
field-sized B-relative index puts the entire commutator [U_c,T₀] in the
common edge seed, including every central attachment. At the actual
two-step vertex, T₀ contains the full radius and the original terminal
subgroup Z_(z-2)(Z_z intersect Q_a). In the noncentral branch a conjugate
of an element of this same commutator moves the critical endpoint and
escapes its penultimate radius. Source (5.9)(*) now also has simultaneous
generation by that same element's whole centralizer and the full endpoint.
The vector-stabilizer argument lifts through the complete natural cover;
source (5.3) retains every central attachment on the original edge seed.
Full-Z fixed indices exclude the hatted-endpoint exception at every original
nonbinary degree. The actual two-step application retains the same T₀,
its whole terminal subgroup and the penultimate-radius escape. The literal
first full-Z orbit span now has T₀-displacement equal to the orbit span of
the complete H, containing that same escaping element. Its full terminal
displacement now vanishes: every nonzero seed displacement would have the
full T₀-image and contain the same escaping element inside that radius.
The actual fixed-intersection argument then bounds endpoint displacement
by the critical commutator. Centralizer generation and the simple quotient
action force the full source H into D_alpha, with all central attachments
retained. In the central-H branch, the entire vertex orbit now has exactly
H as its T₀-displacement. Quotient-image saturation puts H in a terminal
seed and makes H centralize the full endpoint radius. For the original
source conjugator y in G_(a+2), the literal T₂, H₂ and orbit V retain this
displacement and every central attachment. If H₂ moves the endpoint
radius, a shifted critical pair is excluded using the two shifted initial
edges and the unchanged tail; both opposite seeds then lie in each other's
full kernels. For that same y, the entire first orbit now lies in the penultimate and
endpoint kernels. Every conjugator in the full centralizer of H₂ preserves
H₂, so the two literal second spans also lie in those kernels and Q_c.
Backward critical extensions now place the original first two vertices at
the end of a critical path. The terminal intersection proves both original
moving-index lower bounds and supplies an actual escaping seed in T₀.
The source conjugator and terminal path, with simultaneous whole-H₂
centralizer generation and both full second spans, are therefore constructed
without an additional moving-neighbor premise. R₃ centrality, the subsequent
displacement comparison, strict index, terminal exclusions and final
distance-two replacement remain unproved.


